"""
    pw_data_loop_invoke(loop, func, seq, data, size, block, user_data)

invoke func in the context of the thread or in the caller thread when the loop is not running. May be called from the loop's thread, and can be called by multiple threads at the same time.

If called from the loop's thread, all callbacks previously queued with [`pw_data_loop_invoke`](@ref)() will be run synchronously, which might cause unexpected reentrancy problems.

Since 0.3.3

# Arguments
* `loop`:\\[in\\] The loop to invoke func on.
* `func`: The function to be invoked.
* `seq`: A sequence number, opaque to PipeWire. This will be made available to func.
* `data`:\\[in\\] Data that will be copied into the internal ring buffer and made available to func. Because this data is copied, it is okay to pass a pointer to a local variable, but do not pass a pointer to an object that has identity.
* `size`: The size of data to copy.
* `block`: If , do not return until func has been called. Otherwise, returns immediately. Passing  does not risk a deadlock because the data thread is never allowed to wait on any other thread.
* `user_data`: An opaque pointer passed to func.
# Returns
`-EPIPE` if the internal ring buffer filled up, if block is , 0 is returned when seq is [`SPA_ID_INVALID`](@ref) or the sequence number with the ASYNC bit set otherwise. When block is , the return value of func is returned.
### Prototype
```c
int pw_data_loop_invoke(struct pw_data_loop *loop, spa_invoke_func_t func, uint32_t seq, const void *data, size_t size, bool block, void *user_data);
```
"""
function pw_data_loop_invoke(loop, func, seq, data, size, block, user_data)
    @ccall libpipewire_ao.pw_data_loop_invoke(loop::Ptr{pw_data_loop}, func::spa_invoke_func_t, seq::UInt32, data::Ptr{Cvoid}, size::Csize_t, block::Bool, user_data::Ptr{Cvoid})::Cint
end

